1	\documentclass{article}
2	\usepackage{microtype,graphicx,booktabs,amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
3	\usepackage[preprint]{icml2026}
4	\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
5	\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}
6	\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}
7	\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}
8	\newcommand{\norm}[1]{\left\|#1\right\|}
9	\newcommand{\ip}[2]{\langle #1,#2\rangle}
10	\newcommand{\A}{\mathcal{A}}
11	% Preserve the text glyph in both text and mathematical unit labels.
12	\let\textAngstrom\AA
13	\renewcommand{\AA}{\ifmmode\text{\textAngstrom}\else\textAngstrom\fi}
14	\icmltitlerunning{Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty}
15	\begin{document}
16	\twocolumn[
17	\icmltitle{Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits\\for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty}
18	\begin{icmlauthorlist}\icmlauthor{Fourier Cryo Splats Project}{anon}\end{icmlauthorlist}
19	\icmlaffiliation{anon}{Fourier Cryo Splats research project}
20	\icmlcorrespondingauthor{Project repository}{\url{https://github.com/yashpatel5400/fourier-cryo-splats}}
21	\icmlkeywords{cryo-EM, uncertainty quantification, inverse problems, Gaussian splats}
22	\vskip 0.2in]
23	\printAffiliationsAndNotice{}
24	\hypersetup{pdftitle={Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty},pdfsubject={Research manuscript for independent review; not submitted or accepted}}
25	\begin{center}\small\textbf{Research manuscript, 30 September 2026.}\\
26	Under revision after independent review; not submitted.
27	\end{center}
28	\begin{abstract}
29	A confidence interval can exclude density errors that lie outside its reconstruction representation. We study this problem for fixed linear density features in cryogenic electron microscopy (cryo-EM), using continuous Fourier-slice adjoints to audit a declared supported $L^2$ class. The construction specializes classical bias-aware inference. Its operator-specific components are analytic continuous integrals, matrix-free quadrature with explicit integration remainders, and bounds for shared density errors interacting with nonlinear pose uncertainty. Coverage-verification experiments use simulated noise and known simulation limits, with acquisition geometries from three experimental stacks. They expose a precision problem: the pose audit can improve on a no-data interval yet have negligible sign-detection power. A frozen fresh-exposure noise recalibration gives intervals excluding zero for six of twelve coarse experimental features at fixed poses, four with shifts alone, and none with one- or two-degree rotation budgets. These are conditional sensitivities, not experimentally calibrated density coverage. Broader-prior Bayesian controls give favorable fixed-reference results and narrower intervals, without the same uniform-class guarantee. Density radius, support, noise law and pose radii remain assumptions; useful inference at finer structural scales and realistic alignment errors is unestablished.
30	\end{abstract}
31	
32	\section{Introduction}
33	Cryo-EM reconstructs molecular density from noisy projections whose orientations, translations and imaging parameters are imperfectly known. Modern neural and Gaussian representations offer expressive reconstruction models, but expressiveness alone does not establish which reconstructed features the data support. We focus on a fixed local density average or contrast. This target differs from a conformational population, the uncertainty of an atomic model, or prediction of a noisy held-out particle image.
34	
35	An uncertainty calculation usually inherits its reconstruction space. If a Gaussian dictionary spans $S$, a posterior or confidence interval computed in $S$ need not cover density outside $S$. Improving optimization inside the dictionary does not remove this discrepancy. Independent half reconstructions can also share the same representational restriction. Our question is how to audit feature uncertainty outside the reconstruction representation. Gaussian pilots motivate the initial exclusion tests; the final continuous solver operates directly on Fourier-slice adjoints and does not require a Gaussian dictionary.
36	
37	We develop three connected components: a continuous Fourier adjoint-residual audit that separates fitting and inference spaces; bounds for local pose errors, including shared density--pose interactions and an integrated nonlinear remainder; and frozen validation protocols that distinguish fixed-parameter coverage, Bayesian prior-predictive coverage and experimental-image prediction. Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} outlines the conditional procedure. General bias-aware intervals, norm duality, moment inequalities and primal-dual optimization are established tools. The contribution is their explicit continuous Fourier-slice specialization, its computational implementation, and an empirical audit of when the resulting guarantees remain informative.
38	
39	\section{Related Work and Scientific Scope}
40	\begin{figure*}[t]
41	\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/conditional-workflow.pdf}
42	\caption{The conditional procedure. Design choices must be independent of inference noise;
43	weights may be fitted in a convenient representation, while the bias audit uses
44	the declared continuous class. The gap concerns the conservative objective,
45	not the final folded-normal width. A no-data fallback changes both center and
46	width. Experimental consensus poses and class/noise assumptions have not been
47	independently validated here; this diagram does not assert otherwise.}
48	\label{fig:workflow}
49	\end{figure*}
50	\paragraph{Probabilistic reconstruction.} Bayesian inference is foundational in cryo-EM \citep{scheres2012}. Gaussian pseudo-atom and pose MCMC already produces posterior ensembles from preprocessed class averages \citep{joubert2015}. \citet{ullrich2020} already combine differentiable Fourier-slice reconstruction with variational density uncertainty and discuss the distinction between variance and model bias. \citet{rangan2024} examine soft modes of the joint volume/alignment likelihood Hessian. Thus neither a probabilistic Fourier representation nor pose--density ambiguity is a new observation. Our candidate emphasizes a finite-sample, bounded-class statement and a check outside the fitting dictionary.
51	
52	\paragraph{Representations and heterogeneity.} CryoDRGN learns a latent-conditioned density family \citep{zhong2021}; its latent variation is not automatically calibrated uncertainty in a homogeneous density. Ab initio neural inference \citep{zhong2021b} is a different task from fixed-pose backprojection. Gaussian mixtures and Gaussian splatting are also established \citep{chen2021,chen2025,qu2025,shekar2025,gaussianem2026}. We use Fourier kernels as a numerical basis, without treating their existence as the contribution. Continuous-state recovery, conformational populations and pose entanglement are substantial separate problems; the living survey records current RECOVAR, DynaMight, cryoDRGN-AI and CAHRA work with source-level distinctions.
53	
54	\paragraph{Validation and map confidence.} Gold-standard splitting and noise substitution address reconstruction overfitting \citep{schereschen2012,chen2013}; a shell correlation is not a coverage statement for a local feature. BioEM, independent-particle validation and CryoLike establish likelihood-based structural assessment and computation \citep{bioem2013,independent2020,cryolike2025}. LocScale-2.0 calibrates enhancement confidence against a reference target \citep{locscale2026}, a different question from unknown-density confidence using raw particles. Background significance, directional resolution and atomic-model reliability also have distinct targets. Template-based picking can change the selected noise law \citep{balanov2026}; exposure splitting alone does not remove upstream selection.
55	
56	\paragraph{Statistical foundations.} Optimal recovery links linear inverse estimation to bias--variance tradeoffs \citep{donoho1994}. Honest inference over convex classes and bias-aware Gaussian critical values are classical \citep{armstrong2018}. Universal inference also supplies split-likelihood tests with nuisance profiling \citep{universal2020}; it still requires valid likelihoods and appropriate global bounds. Our theorems specialize bias-aware principles and identify cryo-EM assumptions. The living survey distinguishes targeted reading from retrieval.
57	
58	\section{Forward Model and Inference Target}
59	With $F(k)=\int\rho(x)e^{-2\pi i k^\top x}\,dx$, a particle obeys
60	\begin{equation}
61	Y_i(q)=C_i(q)e^{-2\pi i q^\top t_i}F(E_iq)+\epsilon_i(q).
62	\end{equation}
63	$E_i$ embeds a detector plane, $C_i$ is its contrast transfer function (CTF), and frequencies and translations use reciprocal units. We realify one representative of each conjugate Fourier pair and whiten the noise. The initial theory conditions on a fixed design and an independent pilot $\rho_0$, giving
64	\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
65	y-\A\rho_0=\A\delta+Ju+r+\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim N(0,I).
66	\end{equation}
67	Here $\delta=\rho-\rho_0$, $J$ is block diagonal over particles, $u$ describes bounded nuisance errors, and $r$ is a bounded remainder. We assume
68	\begin{equation}\label{eq:class}
69	\norm{\delta}_{H}\le B,\quad\norm{u_i}\le\eta_i,\quad\norm{r_i}\le\gamma_i.
70	\end{equation}
71	$H$ is a declared Gaussian subspace, a supported voxel space, or continuous $L^2$ on a stated cube. These are different classes. A physical density-energy norm includes voxel volume; experiments using an ordinary voxel Euclidean norm explicitly normalize that norm. $B$, $\eta_i$ and $\gamma_i$ are assumptions, not quantities automatically learned from residual fit.
72	
73	The estimand is $t=\ip{\ell}{\rho}$, such as a Gaussian-weighted spatial average or the difference of two such averages. The smoothing scale and coordinate frame are specified in physical units. For weights fixed without the inference noise, use
74	\begin{equation}\label{eq:estimator}
75	\widehat t=\ip{\ell}{\rho_0}+w^\top(y-\A\rho_0).
76	\end{equation}
77	The pilot, dictionary, whitening, target and weights may depend on independent development data. Poses estimated from the inference images violate literal fixed-design independence; supplied consensus poses therefore support only a conditional experimental analysis here.
78	
79	\section{Bias-Aware Confidence and Ambient Auditing}
80	\subsection{A conditional certificate}
81	Define $s=\norm{w}$ and
82	\begin{align}\label{eq:bias}
83	b_H(w)&=B\norm{\ell-\A^*w}_{H}\nonumber\\
84	&\quad+\sum_i\eta_i\norm{J_i^\top w_i}+\sum_i\gamma_i\norm{w_i}.
85	\end{align}
86	Let $q(s,b,\alpha)$ solve
87	\begin{equation}\label{eq:critical}
88	\Phi((q-b)/s)-\Phi((-q-b)/s)=1-\alpha
89	\end{equation}
90	when $s>0$, and set $q(0,b,\alpha)=b$.
91	\begin{theorem}[Conditional coverage]\label{thm:coverage}
92	Under Equations~\eqref{eq:model}--\eqref{eq:class}, the interval
93	$\widehat t\pm q(s,b_H(w),\alpha)$ covers $t$ with probability at least $1-\alpha$, uniformly over the stated class, conditional on the independent design choices.
94	\end{theorem}
95	\begin{proof}
96	The deterministic estimation error is
97	$\ip{\A^*w-\ell}{\delta}+\sum_iw_i^\top J_i u_i+\sum_iw_i^\top r_i$.
98	Cauchy--Schwarz bounds its absolute value by $b_H$. The remaining error is Gaussian with standard deviation $s$. Coverage of a symmetric interval decreases with the absolute Gaussian mean, so the minimum occurs at mean $\pm b_H$ and equals Equation~\eqref{eq:critical}.
99	\end{proof}
100	The result is a direct bias-aware inference construction. It is useful only if its assumptions are defensible and its width improves on the no-data interval $\ip{\ell}{\rho_0}\pm B\norm{\ell}$.
101	
102	\subsection{Why auditing the dictionary matters}
103	Let $S:\R^p\to H$ have orthonormal columns. A restricted calculation replaces the density term by $B\norm{S^*(\ell-\A^*w)}$. Write $h=\ell-\A^*w$. Then
104	\begin{equation}\label{eq:orthogonal}
105	\norm{h}_H^2=\norm{S^*h}^2+\norm{(I-SS^*)h}_H^2.
106	\end{equation}
107	The second term measures uncertainty discarded by the dictionary. It can be large even when the restricted residual is zero. Evaluating the full adjoint residual repairs this omission for $H$ without estimating a representation-error direction from noisy data. It does not cover density outside the declared ambient grid or support.
108	
109	Centering in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimator} also uses the ambient operator. If a surrogate centers the images instead, the discrepancy $w^\top(\A-\widetilde\A)\rho_0$ requires an additional bound. We do not silently substitute a surrogate forward model into the coverage theorem.
110	
111	\subsection{Optimization and verifiable stopping}
112	We optimize the conservative convex objective
113	\begin{equation}\label{eq:objective}
114	\mathcal F_H(w)=z_{1-\alpha/2}\norm{w}+b_H(w),
115	\end{equation}
116	then report the sharper width from Equation~\eqref{eq:critical}. Its dual is
117	\begin{align}
118	\max_{v,a,d}\quad&\ip{\ell}{v}\nonumber\\
119	\text{s.t.}\quad&\norm{v}\le B,\ \norm{a_i}\le\eta_i,\ \norm{d_i}\le\gamma_i,\nonumber\\
120	&\norm{\A v-Ja-d}\le z_{1-\alpha/2}.\label{eq:dual}
121	\end{align}
122	Representing each norm by a Euclidean support function proves this dual; continuity and coercivity yield strong duality. A feasible dual iterate supplies a lower bound before convergence. The gap concerns Equation~\eqref{eq:objective}, not optimality of the folded-normal width.
123	
124	For a reduced density space $S$, a feasible dual vector lifts to $Sv$ in $H$. Thus a restricted dual lower bound and an ambient primal upper bound bracket the same full-space optimum. We enrich $S$ with the component of $\ell-\A^*w$ orthogonal to it, using only design and target information. Restricted exact optima increase under enrichment; individual ambient primal values need not decrease, so we keep the best feasible iterate. No convergence-rate claim is made for this greedy construction.
125	
126	Quadratic majorization gives coefficient-space conjugate-gradient solves with particle-block inverses. A classical Chambolle--Pock implementation is an alternative \citep{chambolle2011}. For fixed poses, a full-space matrix-free comparator solves $(\A^*\A+\lambda I)v=\ell$ within majorization. It can outperform enrichment and is included to prevent an unsupported Gaussian speed claim.
127	
128	\section{Continuous Density and Bounded Pose Errors}
129	\subsection{A Fourier audit independent of the fitting dictionary}
130	For fixed poses, the audit also extends to $H=L^2([-1/2,1/2]^3)$ without a voxel or density bandwidth assumption. This cube is a broader support class than the earlier spherical mask. Its Fourier kernel is $K(v)=\prod_a\operatorname{sinc}(v_a)$. If $c_j=(w_{Rj}+iw_{Ij})C_j/\sigma$ and $f_w(x)=\operatorname{Re}\sum_j c_je^{2\pi i k_j^\top x}$, then
131	\begin{align}
132	\norm{f_w}_H^2=\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}\sum_{j,l}\{&c_j\overline c_l K(k_j-k_l)\nonumber\\
133	&+c_jc_lK(k_j+k_l)\}.
134	\end{align}
135	Gaussian target norms and cross terms have analytic truncated-normal integrals, giving $\norm{\ell-\A^*w}_H$ directly. The continuous objective has a feasible dual vector $v=th$, where $h=\ell-\A^*w$ and $t=\min(B/\norm{h},z/\norm{\A h})$. Its lower bound is $t(\norm{\ell}^2-w^\top\A\ell)$.
136	
137	A matrix-free variant integrates only the finite Fourier adjoint field with Gauss--Legendre quadrature. An explicit quadrature remainder protects both primal and dual bounds; it does not discretize the unknown density class. Standard pivoted Cholesky preconditioning accelerates the solves \citep{harbrecht2012}. The formulas and error derivation are in Appendix~\ref{sec:continuous}. A separate continuous nonlinear-pose extension bounds the norm of the first and second polynomial Fourier fields by padded quadrature Grams. Each particle has a normalized five-dimensional pose vector $u_i$ in the joint unit ball: three rotation coordinates with radius $a$ and two detector shifts with radius $s_t$. This excludes simultaneous maximal rotation and translation. For fixed $w$, first and second adjoint derivatives form five linear and fifteen symmetric quadratic Fourier fields per particle. Their combination at allowed $u_i$ is bounded by a shared-field norm $L_{12}$, using spectral and triangle bounds with integration padding. The unknown density is shared across particles; treating it separately in each image would lose that structure. Finite Gaussian/voxel derivations and alternatives appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:finitemethod}. With $P=\norm{\rho_0}$ and their shared-field bound $L_{12}$, its bias bound is
138	\begin{equation}\label{eq:continuouspose}
139	b\le B\norm{h}+(B+P)\left(L_{12}+\tfrac16\sum_iK_{3i}\norm{w_i}\right).
140	\end{equation}
141	Here $K_{3i}$ uniformly bounds the continuous third-derivative operator. Appendix~\ref{sec:continuouspose} derives the constants and integration error. The frozen studies post-audit fixed-pose weights. The post-review implementation also optimizes a continuous pose-aware objective and tightens the subsequent audit using residual/pose cross terms and exact known-pilot pairings (Appendix~\ref{sec:revision}); it does not claim nonlinear optimality.
142	
143	An integrated-moment refinement replaces the spatial-maximum cubic remainder by
144	\begin{equation}
145	r_{\rm mom}(w)=\frac{B+P}{6}\sum_{i,q}|c_{iq}|\big(L_6^3+3L_4H_4+T_2\big).
146	\end{equation}
147	Here $L_p=2\pi(a\norm{k}c_p+s_t\norm{q})$, $H_p=2\pi a^2\norm{k}c_p$, $T_2=2\pi a^3\norm{k}c_2$, and $(c_2,c_4,c_6)=(12^{-1/2},(3/12^2)^{1/4},(15/12^3)^{1/6})$. These constants bound the cube-integrated moments of linear spatial coordinates. H\"older's inequality controls the exact third phase derivative; retaining actual frequency weights avoids a Hilbert--Schmidt relaxation (Appendix~\ref{sec:momentremainder}). No distribution on the unknown density is introduced.
148	
149	Operationally, we fix the pilot, target, noise model and density/pose classes independently of inference noise; optimize fixed-pose weights; audit their continuous residual and pose derivatives; then use the smallest of the valid remainder bounds. If the resulting interval is wider than the no-data alternative, we switch both center and width to that alternative using design information alone. Only then are inference images used in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimator}. The moment refinement is evaluated in a separate frozen follow-up, preserving the original study.
150	
151	\section{Experiments}
152	Results below distinguish exploratory development from three completed protocols frozen before their respective outcomes: original continuous uncertainty validation, an integrated-moment follow-up, and additional-exposure prediction. Experimental development pools had already been inspected; only the specified frozen follow-ups constitute prospective tests.
153	
154	\subsection{Data, references and provenance}
155	We retain 8,192 extracted experimental images from each of EMPIAR-10028, 10049 and 10076 \citep{empiar10028,empiar10049,empiar10076}. Source exposure groups are reconstructed from deposited metadata. EMPIAR-10076 requires the original Frealign \texttt{FILM} field because its STAR micrograph numbers are sequential particle identifiers. Hash-based whole-group partitions provide pilot, tuning, inference-half and test labels within this development pool. Consensus poses and CTFs remain conditional inputs. EMPIAR-10076 contains heterogeneous assembly intermediates, so a single-density fit is a working consensus model, not experimentally established homogeneity.
156	
157	Deposited maps EMD-2660, EMD-6487 and EMD-8434 define independent voxel signal generators after explicit low-pass resampling. They are simulation truth, not proof of experimental accuracy. Nonuniform Fourier transforms project these maps independently of the Gaussian representation. Native and downloaded-file hashes, source indices, signs, field sizes and software versions are recorded.
158	
159	The physical fields are 482.4, 236.16 and 419.2\,\AA, respectively. Gaussian feature standard deviations of 0.03/0.07 times the field therefore span 7.1--33.8\,\AA. The radius-five inference band reaches wavelengths 47.2--96.5\,\AA; the radius-twelve extension reaches 19.7--40.2\,\AA. These uncertainty experiments probe coarse structural features, not near-atomic reconstruction. The separate image-prediction comparison uses a wider common band on the $64\times64$ working grid.
160	
161	\subsection{Matched controls and exploratory finite-space studies}
162	The numerical controls use identical density functionals and declared units: full/diagonal Gaussian posterior covariance, regularized sampling covariance, joint Gaussian pose covariance, source-group bootstrap, fixed-pose bias-aware intervals and the no-data estimator. Posterior methods pass the matched prior-predictive identity; fixed-parameter boundary coverage is a different criterion. These are mathematical controls, not claims to reproduce every external method's complete implementation.
163	
164	The exploratory studies in Appendix~\ref{sec:finitedevelopment} motivate the continuous extension. A dictionary can exclude measurable bias even when an interval is calibrated in that dictionary. Quadratic pose terms and shared-density spectral bounds improve a first-order relaxation. Weakly regularized full-voxel bootstrap coverage is 0.938/0.956/0.958 with smaller widths than the robust audit; stronger regularization can reduce coverage to 0.339. Thus the audit does not uniformly dominate empirical interval procedures in width. Grid agreement alone does not prove a continuous-density guarantee.
165	
166	\subsection{Frozen continuous implementation and precision checks}
