218	\section{Limitations}
219	The negative pose-audit results concern this procedure and class, not general
220	impossibility of latent-pose reconstruction. Moment methods can recover
221	structure without estimating individual orientations \citep{sharon2020,subspacemom2026}.
222	The current evidence does not establish an end-to-end uncertainty method with estimated poses, noise law and density class. Local pose radii and the total density bound are assumptions. Selecting them from the same inference residuals would require new analysis; a narrow residual does not rule out an almost invisible density error. Learned support can exclude true structure. Gauge and hand ambiguity must be fixed or included in the target class.
223	
224	Our three stacks supply distinct experimental geometries, not ground-truth density coverage labels. The uncertainty simulations use coarse features and prescribed noise; the total density ball includes signed functions and is deliberately broader than molecular structure. Its fixed-pose near-optimality result therefore measures performance in that class, not optimality under positivity or atomic constraints. Pose corrections remain loose, and low sign power limits structural use even when widths improve over no data. Source-group splits do not undo full-data consensus-pose dependence. The shared-density assumption is also unverified: EMPIAR-10076 contains ribosomal assembly intermediates \citep{empiar10076}. Arbitrary calibration means do not extend the inference theorem to view-dependent structural mixtures. Unknown-pose RELION and original-code CryoLike scoring are complete on all three stacks, with every weak reference comparison retained. Full external probabilistic reconstruction pipelines and joint alignment resampling remain unexecuted; mathematical covariance controls are narrower comparisons. The supplied class assumptions and empirical background correlations prevent an end-to-end calibration claim. Simultaneous maps require multiplicity control, and the targeted literature survey is not exhaustive across all cryo-EM applications.
