234	We use smooth conjugate pairs
235	\begin{align}
236	F_c(k)&=\sum_j c_jg_j(k)+\overline c_jg_j(-k),\nonumber\\
237	 g_j(k)&=e^{-\norm{k-\mu_j}^2/(2\sigma_j^2)}.
238	\end{align}
239	This enforces Hermitian symmetry. Hard cutoff kernels from the earlier reconstruction prototype are unsuitable for an unqualified Taylor bound; the uncertainty implementation uses full Gaussians. Their analytic whole-space Gram matrix provides density-energy coordinates $\delta=T\beta$ with $T^*GT=I$, so $\norm{\beta}\le B$ is a density-energy assumption rather than an arbitrary coefficient scale.
240	
241	Parameterize local rotations by a Lie-algebra vector and translations by detector shifts. Normalize their joint vector so $\norm{u_i}\le1$, with physical radii incorporated in the operator. With $D_{ia}=\partial A_i/\partial u_{ia}$ at the pilot pose,
242	\begin{align}
243	A_i(u_i)(c_0+\delta)&=A_i(0)(c_0+\delta)+J_i u_i\nonumber\\
244	&\quad+\sum_a u_{ia}D_{ia}\delta+e_i.\label{eq:pose}
245	\end{align}
246	If $H_i$ uniformly bounds the pilot's second derivative along all allowed segments and $K_i$ bounds the corresponding operator derivative, then $\norm{e_i}\le(H_i+BK_i)/2$. Define $T_i(w_i)=[D_{i1}^\top w_i,\ldots,D_{iq}^\top w_i]$. The interaction contributes at most
247	\begin{equation}
248	B\sum_i\norm{T_i(w_i)}_{\rm op}\le B\sum_i\norm{T_i(w_i)}_{F}.
249	\end{equation}
250	The implemented Frobenius relaxation is a sum of Euclidean group norms. It ignores some sharing of $\delta$ across particles and can be conservative. Charging the entire first-order interaction to an isotropic remainder was substantially worse in development.
251	
252	The same construction operates in the full supported voxel space. For voxel position $x$, box length $d$, rotation radius $a$ and shift radius $s_t$, set
253	\begin{align}
254	L(q,x)&=\tfrac{2\pi}{d}(a\norm{k}\norm{x}+s_t\norm{q}),\\
255	H(q,x)&=\tfrac{2\pi}{d}a^2\norm{k}\norm{x}.
256	\end{align}
257	The absolute second derivative of its complex Fourier column is bounded uniformly by $|C_i(q)|[L(q,x)^2+H(q,x)]$ before whitening. Summing this column bound against $|\rho_0(x)|$ gives the pilot bound; its frequency/voxel Frobenius norm gives the operator bound. Analytic first derivatives supply the full-density interaction. Thus the implemented joint certificate covers the stated ambient density ball and local pose balls simultaneously. Fixed CTFs and known whitened Gaussian noise remain assumptions; CTF error, window correlations and heterogeneous densities are not covered by assertion.
