259	\subsection{Curvature and shared-density post-audits}
260	Retaining the quadratic pose terms replaces the second-order remainder with a uniform cubic one (Appendix~\ref{sec:curvature}). It adds structured pilot and density Hessian terms, and can substantially reduce width. The density component can be tightened further: it is one shared error across particles, not one independent error per image. Let $h=\A^*w-\ell$ and collect blocks $H_0=h$, $H_i=T_i(w)$ and $H_{n+i}=U_i(w)/2$, where $U_i$ contains all ordered second-derivative adjoint columns. Their coefficients are $1,u_i,u_i\otimes u_i$, each of norm at most one. For any positive fixed block scales $d_j$,
261	\begin{equation}\label{eq:shared}
262	\left\|\sum_j H_jv_j\right\|\le
263	\sqrt{\sum_jd_j}\,\big\|[H_j/\sqrt{d_j}]_j\big\|_{\rm op}.
264	\end{equation}
265	Multiplication by $B$ bounds the combined density residual and interactions. The pilot and cubic remainder terms remain unchanged. Equation~\eqref{eq:shared} is an elementary operator-norm inequality. We compare it with separate spectral and triangle bounds, use $d_j=\norm{H_j}_F$ for nonzero blocks, and retain the smallest valid bound. This post-audit uses fixed weights and preserves coverage; it does not inherit an optimality certificate for the new width.
