309	\section{Additional Derivations}
310	\subsection{An identifiability lower bound}
311	\begin{proposition}
312	Let two allowed parameters induce $N(\mu_0,I)$ and $N(\mu_1,I)$, with targets $t_0,t_1$. Any interval covering each target with probability at least $1-\alpha$ has expected length under the first law at least
313	\begin{equation}
314	|t_1-t_0|[1-2\alpha-\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)]_+,
315	\end{equation}
316	where $\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)=2\Phi(\norm{\mu_1-\mu_0}/2)-1$.
317	\end{proposition}
318	\begin{proof}
319	Under $P_0$, the probability of containing $t_1$ is at least $1-\alpha-\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)$ by the definition of total variation. The union bound gives simultaneous containment of both targets with probability at least the displayed positive part. The length on that event is at least their separation. The total variation formula follows by projecting onto the line between the Gaussian means.
320	\end{proof}
321	Two-point expected-length and between-class modulus bounds are classical; see \citet{cailow2004}. This standard two-point argument also applies to different allowed nuisance settings. Exact null directions give a nonvanishing uncertainty floor even with perfect image prediction. It does not assert that a finite numerical search finds the largest possible separation.
322	
323	\begin{corollary}[Fixed-length Gaussian testing bound]\label{cor:fixedlength}
324	Under $y\sim N(\A\rho,I)$ and $\norm{\rho-\rho_0}\le B$, any interval of deterministic length $2q$ covering every target $\ip{\ell}{\rho}$ with probability at least $1-\alpha$, $0<\alpha<1/2$, satisfies, for each nonzero $h\in H$,
325	\begin{equation}
326	q\ge |\ip{\ell}{h}|\min\left\{\frac{B}{\norm{h}},\frac{z_{1-\alpha}}{\norm{\A h}}\right\},
327	\end{equation}
328	where the second ratio is infinite when $\A h=0$.
329	\end{corollary}
330	\begin{proof}
331	Take $\rho_\pm=\rho_0\pm\lambda h$ with $\lambda\norm{h}\le B$ and $\lambda\norm{\A h}<z_{1-\alpha}$. The observation laws have total variation less than $1-2\alpha$. If $2q<2\lambda|\ip{\ell}{h}|$, the two target-containment events are disjoint, whereas their respective coverage probabilities force total variation at least $1-2\alpha$, a contradiction. Taking the limit at the strict boundary proves the result.
332	\end{proof}
333	This is a classical testing argument, not a new minimax theorem. We evaluate $h=\ell-\A^*w$ and $h=\ell$ and retain the larger lower bound. Analytic target integrals and quadrature error bounds evaluate the pairings and norm denominators. The latter use upper bounds, preserving conservatism in real arithmetic; roundoff remains unvalidated. The result concerns deterministic interval length, unlike the preceding expected-length statement. Nominal poses form a submodel of the bounded-pose class, but this lower bound alone cannot establish sharpness for the larger class.
334	
335	The same argument gives a generic efficiency certificate, following the optimal-recovery connection \citep{donoho1994,armstrong2018}. Let $U=\mathcal F_H(w)$ and $L>0$ be a feasible fixed-pose dual value, with $(U-L)/U\le\varepsilon$. Scaling its dual vector by $\kappa=z_{1-\alpha}/z_{1-\alpha/2}$ makes it feasible for the testing bound. Thus the optimum deterministic half-length $q^*$ obeys $q^*\ge\kappa L$, whereas the reported half-width satisfies $q\le U$. Consequently
336	\begin{equation}
337	\frac{q}{q^*}\le\frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}}{z_{1-\alpha}(1-\varepsilon)}.
338	\end{equation}
339	For $\alpha=0.05$ and $\varepsilon=0.005$, this is 1.198. The comparison applies to the continuous fixed-pose class; a post-hoc pose enlargement has no such gap certificate.
