344	\begin{align}
345	P_{jk}&=\left(\frac{2\pi\sigma_j^2\sigma_k^2}{\sigma_j^2+\sigma_k^2}\right)^{3/2}
346	\exp\left[-\frac{\norm{\mu_j-\mu_k}^2}{2(\sigma_j^2+\sigma_k^2)}\right],\\
347	Q_{jk}&=P_{jk}\text{ with }\mu_j-\mu_k\text{ replaced by }\mu_j+\mu_k.\nonumber
348	\end{align}
349	Integrating products of Gaussians and applying Parseval gives real and imaginary coefficient blocks $2(P+Q)$ and $2(P-Q)$, with zero cross block. A zero-centered imaginary coefficient is exactly null. Numerically excluded eigenmodes are reported; exclusion changes the stated Gaussian subspace. In an ambient audit they remain available to the larger space rather than being silently assigned zero uncertainty.
350	
351	\subsection{Uniform nonlinear remainder}
352	For a Gaussian of width $\sigma$ at radial distance $r$, bounds on value, gradient and Hessian are
353	\begin{align}
354	g_0(r)&=e^{-r^2/(2\sigma^2)},\\
355	g_1(r)&=r g_0(r)/\sigma^2,\\
356	g_2(r)&=(1+r^2/\sigma^2)g_0(r)/\sigma^2.
357	\end{align}
358	On an interval of possible distances, $g_0$ is largest at the lower endpoint; $g_1$ and $g_2$ attain their largest value at the point nearest $\sigma$. A rotation radius $a\le\pi$ gives displacement at most $2\norm{k}\sin(a/2)$, speed at most $a\norm{k}$ and acceleration at most $a^2\norm{k}$. A shift radius $s$ gives phase speed at most $2\pi\norm{q}s/\text{box}$.
359	
360	The product rule bounds the second derivative by the sum of squared-speed times $g_2$, acceleration times $g_1$, twice speed times phase-speed times $g_1$, and squared phase-speed times $g_0$. Sum conjugate-pair bounds and apply the fixed CTF/noise scale. A coefficient-column Frobenius bound controls the operator term; the pilot-weighted bound controls its pilot term. Taylor's integral formula gives Equation~\eqref{eq:pose}. Evaluating a Hessian only at the nominal pose would not establish this uniform result.
361

