\icmltitlerunning{Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty}
\begin{document}
\twocolumn[
\icmltitle{Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits\\for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty}
\begin{icmlauthorlist}\icmlauthor{Fourier Cryo Splats Project}{anon}\end{icmlauthorlist}
\icmlaffiliation{anon}{Fourier Cryo Splats research project}
\icmlcorrespondingauthor{Project repository}{\url{https://github.com/yashpatel5400/fourier-cryo-splats}}
\icmlkeywords{cryo-EM, uncertainty quantification, inverse problems, Gaussian splats}
\vskip 0.2in]
\printAffiliationsAndNotice{}
\hypersetup{pdftitle={Continuous Fourier-Slice Audits for Cryo-EM Density Uncertainty},pdfsubject={Research manuscript for independent review; not submitted or accepted}}
\begin{center}\small\textbf{Research manuscript, 30 September 2026.}\\
Under revision after independent review; not submitted.
\end{center}
\begin{abstract}
A confidence interval can exclude density errors that lie outside its reconstruction representation. We study this problem for fixed linear density features in cryogenic electron microscopy (cryo-EM), using continuous Fourier-slice adjoints to audit a declared supported $L^2$ class. The construction specializes classical bias-aware inference. Its operator-specific components are analytic continuous integrals, matrix-free quadrature with explicit integration remainders, and bounds for shared density errors interacting with nonlinear pose uncertainty. Coverage-verification experiments use simulated noise and known simulation limits, with acquisition geometries from three experimental stacks. They expose a precision problem: the pose audit can improve on a no-data interval yet have negligible sign-detection power. A frozen fresh-exposure noise recalibration gives intervals excluding zero for six of twelve coarse experimental features at fixed poses, four with shifts alone, and none with one- or two-degree rotation budgets. These are conditional sensitivities, not experimentally calibrated density coverage. Broader-prior Bayesian controls give favorable fixed-reference results and narrower intervals, without the same uniform-class guarantee. Density radius, support, noise law and pose radii remain assumptions; useful inference at finer structural scales and realistic alignment errors is unestablished.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Cryo-EM reconstructs molecular density from noisy projections whose orientations, translations and imaging parameters are imperfectly known. Modern neural and Gaussian representations offer expressive reconstruction models, but expressiveness alone does not establish which reconstructed features the data support. We focus on a fixed local density average or contrast. This target differs from a conformational population, the uncertainty of an atomic model, or prediction of a noisy held-out particle image.

An uncertainty calculation usually inherits its reconstruction space. If a Gaussian dictionary spans $S$, a posterior or confidence interval computed in $S$ need not cover density outside $S$. Improving optimization inside the dictionary does not remove this discrepancy. Independent half reconstructions can also share the same representational restriction. Our question is how to audit feature uncertainty outside the reconstruction representation. Gaussian pilots motivate the initial exclusion tests; the final continuous solver operates directly on Fourier-slice adjoints and does not require a Gaussian dictionary.

We develop three connected components: a continuous Fourier adjoint-residual audit that separates fitting and inference spaces; bounds for local pose errors, including shared density--pose interactions and an integrated nonlinear remainder; and frozen validation protocols that distinguish fixed-parameter coverage, Bayesian prior-predictive coverage and experimental-image prediction. Figure~\ref{fig:workflow} outlines the conditional procedure. General bias-aware intervals, norm duality, moment inequalities and primal-dual optimization are established tools. The contribution is their explicit continuous Fourier-slice specialization, its computational implementation, and an empirical audit of when the resulting guarantees remain informative.

\section{Related Work and Scientific Scope}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/conditional-workflow.pdf}
\caption{The conditional procedure. Design choices must be independent of inference noise;
weights may be fitted in a convenient representation, while the bias audit uses
the declared continuous class. The gap concerns the conservative objective,
not the final folded-normal width. A no-data fallback changes both center and
width. Experimental consensus poses and class/noise assumptions have not been
independently validated here; this diagram does not assert otherwise.}
\label{fig:workflow}
\end{figure*}
\paragraph{Probabilistic reconstruction.} Bayesian inference is foundational in cryo-EM \citep{scheres2012}. Gaussian pseudo-atom and pose MCMC already produces posterior ensembles from preprocessed class averages \citep{joubert2015}. \citet{ullrich2020} already combine differentiable Fourier-slice reconstruction with variational density uncertainty and discuss the distinction between variance and model bias. \citet{rangan2024} examine soft modes of the joint volume/alignment likelihood Hessian. Thus neither a probabilistic Fourier representation nor pose--density ambiguity is a new observation. Our candidate emphasizes a finite-sample, bounded-class statement and a check outside the fitting dictionary.

\paragraph{Representations and heterogeneity.} CryoDRGN learns a latent-conditioned density family \citep{zhong2021}; its latent variation is not automatically calibrated uncertainty in a homogeneous density. Ab initio neural inference \citep{zhong2021b} is a different task from fixed-pose backprojection. Gaussian mixtures and Gaussian splatting are also established \citep{chen2021,chen2025,qu2025,shekar2025,gaussianem2026}. We use Fourier kernels as a numerical basis, without treating their existence as the contribution. Continuous-state recovery, conformational populations and pose entanglement are substantial separate problems; the living survey records current RECOVAR, DynaMight, cryoDRGN-AI and CAHRA work with source-level distinctions.

\paragraph{Validation and map confidence.} Gold-standard splitting and noise substitution address reconstruction overfitting \citep{schereschen2012,chen2013}; a shell correlation is not a coverage statement for a local feature. BioEM, independent-particle validation and CryoLike establish likelihood-based structural assessment and computation \citep{bioem2013,independent2020,cryolike2025}. LocScale-2.0 calibrates enhancement confidence against a reference target \citep{locscale2026}, a different question from unknown-density confidence using raw particles. Background significance, directional resolution and atomic-model reliability also have distinct targets. Template-based picking can change the selected noise law \citep{balanov2026}; exposure splitting alone does not remove upstream selection.

\paragraph{Statistical foundations.} Optimal recovery links linear inverse estimation to bias--variance tradeoffs \citep{donoho1994}. Honest inference over convex classes and bias-aware Gaussian critical values are classical \citep{armstrong2018}. Universal inference also supplies split-likelihood tests with nuisance profiling \citep{universal2020}; it still requires valid likelihoods and appropriate global bounds. Our theorems specialize bias-aware principles and identify cryo-EM assumptions. The living survey distinguishes targeted reading from retrieval.

\section{Forward Model and Inference Target}
With $F(k)=\int\rho(x)e^{-2\pi i k^\top x}\,dx$, a particle obeys
\begin{equation}
Y_i(q)=C_i(q)e^{-2\pi i q^\top t_i}F(E_iq)+\epsilon_i(q).
\end{equation}
$E_i$ embeds a detector plane, $C_i$ is its contrast transfer function (CTF), and frequencies and translations use reciprocal units. We realify one representative of each conjugate Fourier pair and whiten the noise. The initial theory conditions on a fixed design and an independent pilot $\rho_0$, giving
\begin{equation}\label{eq:model}
y-\A\rho_0=\A\delta+Ju+r+\epsilon,\quad\epsilon\sim N(0,I).
\end{equation}
Here $\delta=\rho-\rho_0$, $J$ is block diagonal over particles, $u$ describes bounded nuisance errors, and $r$ is a bounded remainder. We assume
\begin{equation}\label{eq:class}
\norm{\delta}_{H}\le B,\quad\norm{u_i}\le\eta_i,\quad\norm{r_i}\le\gamma_i.
\end{equation}
$H$ is a declared Gaussian subspace, a supported voxel space, or continuous $L^2$ on a stated cube. These are different classes. A physical density-energy norm includes voxel volume; experiments using an ordinary voxel Euclidean norm explicitly normalize that norm. $B$, $\eta_i$ and $\gamma_i$ are assumptions, not quantities automatically learned from residual fit.

The estimand is $t=\ip{\ell}{\rho}$, such as a Gaussian-weighted spatial average or the difference of two such averages. The smoothing scale and coordinate frame are specified in physical units. For weights fixed without the inference noise, use
\begin{equation}\label{eq:estimator}
\widehat t=\ip{\ell}{\rho_0}+w^\top(y-\A\rho_0).
\end{equation}
The pilot, dictionary, whitening, target and weights may depend on independent development data. Poses estimated from the inference images violate literal fixed-design independence; supplied consensus poses therefore support only a conditional experimental analysis here.

\section{Bias-Aware Confidence and Ambient Auditing}
\subsection{A conditional certificate}
Define $s=\norm{w}$ and
\begin{align}\label{eq:bias}
b_H(w)&=B\norm{\ell-\A^*w}_{H}\nonumber\\
&\quad+\sum_i\eta_i\norm{J_i^\top w_i}+\sum_i\gamma_i\norm{w_i}.
\end{align}
Let $q(s,b,\alpha)$ solve
\begin{equation}\label{eq:critical}
\Phi((q-b)/s)-\Phi((-q-b)/s)=1-\alpha
\end{equation}
when $s>0$, and set $q(0,b,\alpha)=b$.
\begin{theorem}[Conditional coverage]\label{thm:coverage}
Under Equations~\eqref{eq:model}--\eqref{eq:class}, the interval
$\widehat t\pm q(s,b_H(w),\alpha)$ covers $t$ with probability at least $1-\alpha$, uniformly over the stated class, conditional on the independent design choices.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The deterministic estimation error is
$\ip{\A^*w-\ell}{\delta}+\sum_iw_i^\top J_i u_i+\sum_iw_i^\top r_i$.
Cauchy--Schwarz bounds its absolute value by $b_H$. The remaining error is Gaussian with standard deviation $s$. Coverage of a symmetric interval decreases with the absolute Gaussian mean, so the minimum occurs at mean $\pm b_H$ and equals Equation~\eqref{eq:critical}.
\end{proof}
The result is a direct bias-aware inference construction. It is useful only if its assumptions are defensible and its width improves on the no-data interval $\ip{\ell}{\rho_0}\pm B\norm{\ell}$.

\subsection{Why auditing the dictionary matters}
Let $S:\R^p\to H$ have orthonormal columns. A restricted calculation replaces the density term by $B\norm{S^*(\ell-\A^*w)}$. Write $h=\ell-\A^*w$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:orthogonal}
\norm{h}_H^2=\norm{S^*h}^2+\norm{(I-SS^*)h}_H^2.
\end{equation}
The second term measures uncertainty discarded by the dictionary. It can be large even when the restricted residual is zero. Evaluating the full adjoint residual repairs this omission for $H$ without estimating a representation-error direction from noisy data. It does not cover density outside the declared ambient grid or support.

Centering in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimator} also uses the ambient operator. If a surrogate centers the images instead, the discrepancy $w^\top(\A-\widetilde\A)\rho_0$ requires an additional bound. We do not silently substitute a surrogate forward model into the coverage theorem.

\subsection{Optimization and verifiable stopping}
We optimize the conservative convex objective
\begin{equation}\label{eq:objective}
\mathcal F_H(w)=z_{1-\alpha/2}\norm{w}+b_H(w),
\end{equation}
then report the sharper width from Equation~\eqref{eq:critical}. Its dual is
\begin{align}
\max_{v,a,d}\quad&\ip{\ell}{v}\nonumber\\
\text{s.t.}\quad&\norm{v}\le B,\ \norm{a_i}\le\eta_i,\ \norm{d_i}\le\gamma_i,\nonumber\\
&\norm{\A v-Ja-d}\le z_{1-\alpha/2}.\label{eq:dual}
\end{align}
Representing each norm by a Euclidean support function proves this dual; continuity and coercivity yield strong duality. A feasible dual iterate supplies a lower bound before convergence. The gap concerns Equation~\eqref{eq:objective}, not optimality of the folded-normal width.

For a reduced density space $S$, a feasible dual vector lifts to $Sv$ in $H$. Thus a restricted dual lower bound and an ambient primal upper bound bracket the same full-space optimum. We enrich $S$ with the component of $\ell-\A^*w$ orthogonal to it, using only design and target information. Restricted exact optima increase under enrichment; individual ambient primal values need not decrease, so we keep the best feasible iterate. No convergence-rate claim is made for this greedy construction.

Quadratic majorization gives coefficient-space conjugate-gradient solves with particle-block inverses. A classical Chambolle--Pock implementation is an alternative \citep{chambolle2011}. For fixed poses, a full-space matrix-free comparator solves $(\A^*\A+\lambda I)v=\ell$ within majorization. It can outperform enrichment and is included to prevent an unsupported Gaussian speed claim.

\section{Continuous Density and Bounded Pose Errors}
\subsection{A Fourier audit independent of the fitting dictionary}
For fixed poses, the audit also extends to $H=L^2([-1/2,1/2]^3)$ without a voxel or density bandwidth assumption. This cube is a broader support class than the earlier spherical mask. Its Fourier kernel is $K(v)=\prod_a\operatorname{sinc}(v_a)$. If $c_j=(w_{Rj}+iw_{Ij})C_j/\sigma$ and $f_w(x)=\operatorname{Re}\sum_j c_je^{2\pi i k_j^\top x}$, then
\begin{align}
\norm{f_w}_H^2=\tfrac12\operatorname{Re}\sum_{j,l}\{&c_j\overline c_l K(k_j-k_l)\nonumber\\
&+c_jc_lK(k_j+k_l)\}.
\end{align}
Gaussian target norms and cross terms have analytic truncated-normal integrals, giving $\norm{\ell-\A^*w}_H$ directly. The continuous objective has a feasible dual vector $v=th$, where $h=\ell-\A^*w$ and $t=\min(B/\norm{h},z/\norm{\A h})$. Its lower bound is $t(\norm{\ell}^2-w^\top\A\ell)$.

A matrix-free variant integrates only the finite Fourier adjoint field with Gauss--Legendre quadrature. An explicit quadrature remainder protects both primal and dual bounds; it does not discretize the unknown density class. Standard pivoted Cholesky preconditioning accelerates the solves \citep{harbrecht2012}. The formulas and error derivation are in Appendix~\ref{sec:continuous}. A separate continuous nonlinear-pose extension bounds the norm of the first and second polynomial Fourier fields by padded quadrature Grams. Each particle has a normalized five-dimensional pose vector $u_i$ in the joint unit ball: three rotation coordinates with radius $a$ and two detector shifts with radius $s_t$. This excludes simultaneous maximal rotation and translation. For fixed $w$, first and second adjoint derivatives form five linear and fifteen symmetric quadratic Fourier fields per particle. Their combination at allowed $u_i$ is bounded by a shared-field norm $L_{12}$, using spectral and triangle bounds with integration padding. The unknown density is shared across particles; treating it separately in each image would lose that structure. Finite Gaussian/voxel derivations and alternatives appear in Appendix~\ref{sec:finitemethod}. With $P=\norm{\rho_0}$ and their shared-field bound $L_{12}$, its bias bound is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:continuouspose}
b\le B\norm{h}+(B+P)\left(L_{12}+\tfrac16\sum_iK_{3i}\norm{w_i}\right).
\end{equation}
Here $K_{3i}$ uniformly bounds the continuous third-derivative operator. Appendix~\ref{sec:continuouspose} derives the constants and integration error. The frozen studies post-audit fixed-pose weights. The post-review implementation also optimizes a continuous pose-aware objective and tightens the subsequent audit using residual/pose cross terms and exact known-pilot pairings (Appendix~\ref{sec:revision}); it does not claim nonlinear optimality.

An integrated-moment refinement replaces the spatial-maximum cubic remainder by
\begin{equation}
r_{\rm mom}(w)=\frac{B+P}{6}\sum_{i,q}|c_{iq}|\big(L_6^3+3L_4H_4+T_2\big).
\end{equation}
Here $L_p=2\pi(a\norm{k}c_p+s_t\norm{q})$, $H_p=2\pi a^2\norm{k}c_p$, $T_2=2\pi a^3\norm{k}c_2$, and $(c_2,c_4,c_6)=(12^{-1/2},(3/12^2)^{1/4},(15/12^3)^{1/6})$. These constants bound the cube-integrated moments of linear spatial coordinates. H\"older's inequality controls the exact third phase derivative; retaining actual frequency weights avoids a Hilbert--Schmidt relaxation (Appendix~\ref{sec:momentremainder}). No distribution on the unknown density is introduced.

Operationally, we fix the pilot, target, noise model and density/pose classes independently of inference noise; optimize fixed-pose weights; audit their continuous residual and pose derivatives; then use the smallest of the valid remainder bounds. If the resulting interval is wider than the no-data alternative, we switch both center and width to that alternative using design information alone. Only then are inference images used in Equation~\eqref{eq:estimator}. The moment refinement is evaluated in a separate frozen follow-up, preserving the original study.

\section{Experiments}
Results below distinguish exploratory development from three completed protocols frozen before their respective outcomes: original continuous uncertainty validation, an integrated-moment follow-up, and additional-exposure prediction. Experimental development pools had already been inspected; only the specified frozen follow-ups constitute prospective tests.

\subsection{Data, references and provenance}
We retain 8,192 extracted experimental images from each of EMPIAR-10028, 10049 and 10076 \citep{empiar10028,empiar10049,empiar10076}. Source exposure groups are reconstructed from deposited metadata. EMPIAR-10076 requires the original Frealign \texttt{FILM} field because its STAR micrograph numbers are sequential particle identifiers. Hash-based whole-group partitions provide pilot, tuning, inference-half and test labels within this development pool. Consensus poses and CTFs remain conditional inputs. EMPIAR-10076 contains heterogeneous assembly intermediates, so a single-density fit is a working consensus model, not experimentally established homogeneity.

Deposited maps EMD-2660, EMD-6487 and EMD-8434 define independent voxel signal generators after explicit low-pass resampling. They are simulation truth, not proof of experimental accuracy. Nonuniform Fourier transforms project these maps independently of the Gaussian representation. Native and downloaded-file hashes, source indices, signs, field sizes and software versions are recorded.

The physical fields are 482.4, 236.16 and 419.2\,\AA, respectively. Gaussian feature standard deviations of 0.03/0.07 times the field therefore span 7.1--33.8\,\AA. The radius-five inference band reaches wavelengths 47.2--96.5\,\AA; the radius-twelve extension reaches 19.7--40.2\,\AA. These uncertainty experiments probe coarse structural features, not near-atomic reconstruction. The separate image-prediction comparison uses a wider common band on the $64\times64$ working grid.

\subsection{Matched controls and exploratory finite-space studies}
The numerical controls use identical density functionals and declared units: full/diagonal Gaussian posterior covariance, regularized sampling covariance, joint Gaussian pose covariance, source-group bootstrap, fixed-pose bias-aware intervals and the no-data estimator. Posterior methods pass the matched prior-predictive identity; fixed-parameter boundary coverage is a different criterion. These are mathematical controls, not claims to reproduce every external method's complete implementation.

The exploratory studies in Appendix~\ref{sec:finitedevelopment} motivate the continuous extension. A dictionary can exclude measurable bias even when an interval is calibrated in that dictionary. Quadratic pose terms and shared-density spectral bounds improve a first-order relaxation. Weakly regularized full-voxel bootstrap coverage is 0.938/0.956/0.958 with smaller widths than the robust audit; stronger regularization can reduce coverage to 0.339. Thus the audit does not uniformly dominate empirical interval procedures in width. Grid agreement alone does not prove a continuous-density guarantee.

\subsection{Frozen continuous implementation and precision checks}
The first protocol was frozen at commit \texttt{76dbd53} before its outcomes. Four additional-cohort geometry subsets per stack, two feature scales, two targets and two pose budgets give 96 audit settings and 2,304 signal/scenario records. Each subset contains 128 particles; its prescribed density radius is $B=2$, and the simulated Gaussian noise scale is fixed from a pilot SNR convention. Four unit-norm cell generators include a deposited map, ellipsoid unions, modulated shells and signed multiscale fields. Exact continuous-density boundaries exercise directions outside the cell representation. The protocol includes nominal, random-boundary and coherent-boundary poses, plus separately reported fourfold pose exceedance and doubled noise scale. Acquisition subsets may share exposure groups; they are not independent biological replicates.

All 48 unique fixed-pose fits meet the 0.5\% sum-objective gap. Across all 1,536 in-class signal/scenario records, minimum analytic coverage, used here as an implementation check, is 0.99377 for the continuous pose audit, 0.93922 for the fixed-pose audit and zero for the 24-cell projection audit. The cell-defined generators are covered conservatively by both audited fixed-pose procedures; the severe cell-audit failures occur at explicitly continuous boundaries. These are worst-case implementation checks, not typical-map failure or experimental calibration estimates. Scalar Gaussian draws provide only a numerical cross-check and are retained in the historical records.

Figure~\ref{fig:frozenuncertainty} shows all selected designs. Broad-feature relative widths are 0.109--0.175 at $0.1^\circ$ and 0.271--0.457 at $0.5^\circ$. Fine-feature widths rise to 0.759--0.779 and 0.885--0.998, respectively, nearly reaching the no-data alternative. None of the 96 original-audit settings switches to no data. The pose audit's minimum calculated coverage across the 768 model-violation records is 0.99948. Its conservatism prevents those selected severities from exposing a failure; this supplies no robustness or calibration claim. Sign power, marginal Monte Carlo intervals, all methods and every failure remain in the released records.

\subsection{Frozen integrated-moment follow-up}
The follow-up was frozen at \texttt{8d66785} after the refinement's development, with fresh signal/noise seeds and particle subsets disjoint from the original study. Its 48 settings use broad targets and $1^\circ/2^\circ$ budgets. All 24 unique fits converge within the 0.5\% gap. Across 768 in-class records, the refined audit's analytic coverage rounds to one in float64, whereas the fixed-pose audit reaches 0.67114 and the cell-projected audit 0.12596. The refined intervals are also too conservative for the 384 selected model-violation records to expose failure.

The spatial-maximum bound uses no data in all 48 settings; integrated moments reduce this to 16 (Table~\ref{tab:frozenmoments}). All $1^\circ$ widths are 0.304--0.501 of no data. At $2^\circ$, only EMPIAR-10076 remains narrower, at 0.866--0.987. However, correct-sign power for the in-class cell generators is at most $2.3\times10^{-5}$; constructed continuous boundaries attain at most 0.5184. Thus the gain in interval width is real but does not demonstrate useful detection in these map-like signals. Figure~\ref{fig:frozenmoments} and released records retain all settings.
\input{frozen-moments-results.tex}

\subsection{Continuous audit and assumption failures}
The continuous fixed-pose comparison uses the same three acquisition geometries, two targets and two averaging scales. Pilots and independent map generators are orthonormal constant-cell functions on grids 24 and 64, respectively. Their exact Fourier transforms include cell sinc factors and a half-cell phase; target integrals use normal-CDF differences. Both have unit continuous $L^2$ norm, so the same prescribed $B=2$ covers their difference. The inference class itself contains all signed $L^2$ functions on the cube; no positivity or atomic-structure restriction is imposed.

All 12 dense continuous solves meet the 0.5\% sum-objective gap, and independently evaluated matrix-free results agree to numerical tolerance. Figure~\ref{fig:continuous} distinguishes the continuous bound from projections onto finite cell spaces. At a constructed continuous bias boundary, small relative width differences can matter: a 64-cell interval at the EMPIAR-10028 broad central estimator has coverage approximately 0.923 despite a width difference of only 1.7\%. The independent map's coverage remains one; this adversary is not a typical-signal claim. Refitting weights for the larger cube class is essential: transferring sphere-only weights can be much less informative.

A classical Gaussian two-point lower bound supplies an additional design-only efficiency check (Corollary~\ref{cor:fixedlength}). Across all 18 low- and higher-band fixed-pose targets, its necessary half-length is 0.840--0.882 of the reported interval half-width. Thus the fixed-pose procedures are within a factor 1.20 of the minimum possible deterministic interval length under the stated continuous class, subject to the reported numerical accuracy. This compares against arbitrary interval centers, but not variable-length intervals. The factor is consistent with the classical testing/duality connection below; it is not a new minimax principle. It does not imply that the additional pose bound is comparably sharp.

The first 128-particle timing comparison uses about 419\,MB for dense Gram arrays, versus 86\,MB for quadrature plus a 1,024-column preconditioner. Setup plus solve/audit takes approximately 14 versus 6 seconds under concurrent Mac load; these stored-array counts are not peak resident memory. An initial weak preconditioner was substantially slower and is retained. The matrix-free implementation also completed both broad targets with 1,024 particles for all three geometries. A separate radius-12 continuous comparison completes both fine targets on all three geometries: relative widths range from 0.120 to 0.238, with all six sum-objective gaps below 0.5\%. Individual solve/audit times range from 483 to 1,223 seconds. Despite improvement over no data, reference-generator sign power is below $1.1\times10^{-9}$ in every fine-target case. These results do not establish biologically useful fine-feature detection. Projecting the same residual onto 64-cell densities yields coverage 0.791--0.894 at the constructed continuous boundary, despite widths only 3.7--6.5\% smaller.

The joint continuous-pose post-audit is complete for both broad targets, all three geometries and rotation budgets $0.1^\circ/0.5^\circ$, with a joint shift budget of $0.01/24$ field units (0.201, 0.098 and 0.175\,\AA, respectively). These are very small translation sensitivities, not empirically calibrated alignment errors. Its twelve relative half-widths range from 0.110 to 0.444 (Figure~\ref{fig:continuouspose}). Exact nonlinear constant-cell projections give reference coverage at least 0.99999999998 in these tests, revealing conservatism rather than empirical calibration. At one nonzero coherent pose per case, analytic sinc integrals eliminate the entire continuous density ball; its feasible bias is 0.24--0.86 of the uniform upper bound. These are lower bounds on worst bias, not globally optimized adversaries. Each audit takes about 38--40 seconds and stores approximately 725\,MB of field/Gram arrays under concurrent Mac load.

Six explicit assumption-stress runs each retain 273 records (Figure~\ref{fig:assumptions}). Support-only violations preserve the full density-energy radius, isolating the damage caused by an incorrect mask. View-coupled two-state mixtures preserve an equal-population mean but violate homogeneity. Both can cause severe undercoverage, as can correlated noise and understated noise scale. The tested low-bandwidth defocus offsets and elliptical Student-$t$ controls do not cause undercoverage for the shared-density pose bounds. Every severity setting and unfavorable result is retained.

A wider development sweep exposes the original continuous bound's limits: every broad target falls back to no data at $1^\circ$, $2^\circ$ and $5^\circ$. The integrated-moment refinement reduces cubic remainder bounds by 6.79--14.74 times across all 30 small- and large-angle settings, without changing weights. No-data fallbacks decrease from 18 to 10; all six $1^\circ$ intervals have relative widths 0.300--0.532 (Figure~\ref{fig:momentremainder}). This is post-hoc development, not the frozen study's outcome. The remaining gap to feasible nonlinear biases and the large-angle failures are retained.

A stronger continuous stress search uses three starts for each bias sign, both targets, all three geometries and $0.5^\circ/1^\circ/2^\circ$ budgets. All 108 final candidates are recomputed with analytic continuous-density integrals and exact nonlinear poses in float64 after an accelerated quadrature-surrogate search. The strongest feasible bias in each setting is 1.06--2.54 times its coherent-pose control, but only 0.16--0.57 of the integrated upper bound (Figure~\ref{fig:continuousadversaries}). None exceeds that bound. These local searches expose remaining slack; they neither prove a global maximum nor supply validated floating-point lower bounds.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fresh-noise-targets.pdf}
\caption{All locked-feature intervals after frozen fresh-exposure noise recalibration. Each stack has three pilot-selected regions and one matched center. Colors indicate separate pose-class sensitivity analyses, each with a twelve-feature multiplicity budget; shifts have radius 0.5\,\AA. Intervals and approximate-map values are normalized by the same feature's no-data half-width. The plotted reference uses the historical unregistered frame; Appendix~\ref{sec:referenceframe} retains the registered comparison. Reference inclusion is not experimental density coverage. EMPIAR-10076 is heterogeneous, so its shared-density model is a conditional consensus approximation.}
\label{fig:freshtargets}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Baselines and numerical checks}
Implemented matched mathematical baselines include full Gaussian posterior covariance, diagonal variational covariance, regularized sampling covariance, joint Gaussian pose covariance, identifiable unregularized estimation, fixed-pose bias-aware intervals, coarse and structured nuisance bounds, and the no-data interval. These are not executions of every external paper's original code. Full Gaussian posteriors pass their exactly matched linear prior-predictive identity at 0.95. Narrow priors can nevertheless fail bounded fixed-signal tests; wider priors and unregularized estimation can produce very wide intervals.

Independent conic optimization checks the majorization, primal-dual and ambient solvers on small instances. Derivative finite differences, nonlinear remainder tests, direct Fourier sums, adjoint identities and independent Gaussian-integral checks validate implementation components. The passing test suite includes a three-dimensional Fourier-transform check for externally generated maps. Tight FINUFFT tolerances are numerical checks, not interval-arithmetic guarantees on roundoff.

\subsection{Locked targets and fresh noise recalibration}
After development, we lock three pilot-selected regions and one matched central feature per stack, all with Gaussian standard deviation 20\,\AA. All twelve radius-12, 128-particle fixed-pose fits satisfy the declared 0.5\% objective-gap criterion. In the original unregistered reference simulations, sign power exceeds 0.99999 except for one EMPIAR-10049 region (0.0158); pilot-only registration raises that case to 0.9563 without changing its estimator. All nonlinear audits and experimental applications are complete; none of the one-/two-degree audits yields useful reference sign power.

The twelve estimators and 116 dependency files were published at \texttt{60efd9b} before downloading a reserved calibration cohort: one particle from each of 128 previously unused exposures per stack. A Gaussian directional-noise bound permits arbitrary calibration means and arbitrary within-exposure inference dependence; independence between exposures and a common marginal noise covariance remain assumptions. Each pose class has its own twelve-feature family budget. Fresh standard-deviation upper bounds are 0.729--0.955 of the old bounds. Figure~\ref{fig:freshtargets} shows all 48 intervals: six fixed-pose features and four shift-only features exclude zero, but none with one- or two-degree rotation budgets. All approximate-map values lie inside, which does not establish density coverage. A later centered-calibration alternative raises fixed/shift-only zero exclusions to ten/six, with rotational exclusions still zero (Appendix~\ref{sec:referenceframe}). The inference images had already been inspected, and calibration does not establish the pose, density-class or homogeneity assumptions. Appendix~\ref{sec:pilotselected} gives the full protocol and results.



The matched broader-prior Fourier Gaussian baseline achieves fixed-reference coverage 0.9872--0.9998 and relative widths 0.016--0.074 at nominal poses. These favorable results concern particular signals rather than a uniform density class. Local Gaussian pose marginalization increases widths by only 0.0066--0.2875\%, but omits density--pose products. Neither comparison establishes experimental calibration.

\paragraph{Further pose development.} A matrix-free shared-field operator completes a 10,000-particle audit at 1.95\,GB peak memory. All eighteen pose-aware cases are complete, but their two-degree widths remain 0.427--0.633 of no data with negligible power. A selected cubic-weight fit reduces a one-degree width from 0.473 to 0.180. Pilot-only registration changes its simulated minimum sign power from 0.00654 to 0.95725 without changing weights, but its experimental interval still contains zero and its relative surrogate gap remains 0.940. A joint design improves width by 2.55\%, but its centered experimental interval $2.469\pm3.011$ still contains zero (Appendix~\ref{sec:referenceframe}). Declared CTF errors make the higher-band triangle audit largely vacuous. Appendix~\ref{sec:revision} retains these limitations and the unsuccessful coordinate-metric follow-up. Appendix~\ref{sec:mixturedevelopment} records a separate pose-marginal validation direction whose continuous likelihood bounds remain unresolved.

\section{Limitations}
The negative pose-audit results concern this procedure and class, not general
impossibility of latent-pose reconstruction. Moment methods can recover
structure without estimating individual orientations \citep{sharon2020,subspacemom2026}.
The current evidence does not establish an end-to-end uncertainty method with estimated poses, noise law and density class. Local pose radii and the total density bound are assumptions. Selecting them from the same inference residuals would require new analysis; a narrow residual does not rule out an almost invisible density error. Learned support can exclude true structure. Gauge and hand ambiguity must be fixed or included in the target class.

Our three stacks supply distinct experimental geometries, not ground-truth density coverage labels. The uncertainty simulations use coarse features and prescribed noise; the total density ball includes signed functions and is deliberately broader than molecular structure. Its fixed-pose near-optimality result therefore measures performance in that class, not optimality under positivity or atomic constraints. Pose corrections remain loose, and low sign power limits structural use even when widths improve over no data. Source-group splits do not undo full-data consensus-pose dependence. The shared-density assumption is also unverified: EMPIAR-10076 contains ribosomal assembly intermediates \citep{empiar10076}. Arbitrary calibration means do not extend the inference theorem to view-dependent structural mixtures. Unknown-pose RELION and original-code CryoLike scoring are complete on all three stacks, with every weak reference comparison retained. Full external probabilistic reconstruction pipelines and joint alignment resampling remain unexecuted; mathematical covariance controls are narrower comparisons. The supplied class assumptions and empirical background correlations prevent an end-to-end calibration claim. Simultaneous maps require multiplicity control, and the targeted literature survey is not exhaustive across all cryo-EM applications.

\section*{Reproducibility and Impact}
The active research branch is \texttt{uncertainty-study} at \url{https://github.com/yashpatel5400/fourier-cryo-splats}. It preserves failed development approaches and distinguishes them from completed outputs. The earlier reconstruction release v0.1.0 remains available with its conditional FSC results; it is not the uncertainty study's accuracy claim. No new conference submission or acceptance is implied by this format. Background PDFs are retained locally with source hashes rather than redistributed. Prematurely calibrated-looking maps can mislead structural interpretation; explicit assumptions and negative controls are central outputs of this work.

