\subsection{Bootstrap tradeoff and finite-grid stability}
A fixed-pose benchmark fits 33- and 123-mode Gaussian models and all 2,517 supported voxels. It uses 999 source-group bootstrap resamples, four prescribed ridge strengths and 1,000 independent Gaussian measurement-noise replications per setting. Percentile/basic intervals, sampling covariance, posterior covariance and a same-estimator ambient bias audit are recorded. The group-resampling plan is reused across noise draws; binomial error bars are conditional on that plan.

Weakly regularized full-voxel bootstrap intervals have center coverage 0.938, 0.956 and 0.958 across the three geometries, with widths smaller than the robust audit. Strong regularization can cause substantial undercoverage: EMPIAR-10028 center coverage drops to approximately 0.339. A conservative bias audit therefore does not dominate bootstrap width in every setting. Figure~\ref{fig:bootstrap} retains the regularization sweep, including cases favorable to bootstrap.

A fixed-pose refinement study audits 48 coarse-grid solutions on a common $64^3$ supported space. Grids 16, 24, 32 and 48 preserve physical field, density-energy norm and noise units. Width ratios between fine audit and original calculation range from 0.72--1.45 at grid 16 and approximately 0.999--1.006 at grid 32. Nonlinear Fourier-moment audits additionally cover both targets, both pose radii and all three geometries on grids 24, 32 and 64. Discretizations are not identical nested classes; ratios need not increase monotonically. Agreement is a numerical sensitivity check, not a continuous-density guarantee.


\section{Additional Derivations}
\subsection{An identifiability lower bound}
\begin{proposition}
Let two allowed parameters induce $N(\mu_0,I)$ and $N(\mu_1,I)$, with targets $t_0,t_1$. Any interval covering each target with probability at least $1-\alpha$ has expected length under the first law at least
\begin{equation}
|t_1-t_0|[1-2\alpha-\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)]_+,
\end{equation}
where $\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)=2\Phi(\norm{\mu_1-\mu_0}/2)-1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Under $P_0$, the probability of containing $t_1$ is at least $1-\alpha-\operatorname{TV}(P_0,P_1)$ by the definition of total variation. The union bound gives simultaneous containment of both targets with probability at least the displayed positive part. The length on that event is at least their separation. The total variation formula follows by projecting onto the line between the Gaussian means.
\end{proof}
Two-point expected-length and between-class modulus bounds are classical; see \citet{cailow2004}. This standard two-point argument also applies to different allowed nuisance settings. Exact null directions give a nonvanishing uncertainty floor even with perfect image prediction. It does not assert that a finite numerical search finds the largest possible separation.

\begin{corollary}[Fixed-length Gaussian testing bound]\label{cor:fixedlength}
Under $y\sim N(\A\rho,I)$ and $\norm{\rho-\rho_0}\le B$, any interval of deterministic length $2q$ covering every target $\ip{\ell}{\rho}$ with probability at least $1-\alpha$, $0<\alpha<1/2$, satisfies, for each nonzero $h\in H$,
\begin{equation}
q\ge |\ip{\ell}{h}|\min\left\{\frac{B}{\norm{h}},\frac{z_{1-\alpha}}{\norm{\A h}}\right\},
\end{equation}
where the second ratio is infinite when $\A h=0$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Take $\rho_\pm=\rho_0\pm\lambda h$ with $\lambda\norm{h}\le B$ and $\lambda\norm{\A h}<z_{1-\alpha}$. The observation laws have total variation less than $1-2\alpha$. If $2q<2\lambda|\ip{\ell}{h}|$, the two target-containment events are disjoint, whereas their respective coverage probabilities force total variation at least $1-2\alpha$, a contradiction. Taking the limit at the strict boundary proves the result.
\end{proof}
This is a classical testing argument, not a new minimax theorem. We evaluate $h=\ell-\A^*w$ and $h=\ell$ and retain the larger lower bound. Analytic target integrals and quadrature error bounds evaluate the pairings and norm denominators. The latter use upper bounds, preserving conservatism in real arithmetic; roundoff remains unvalidated. The result concerns deterministic interval length, unlike the preceding expected-length statement. Nominal poses form a submodel of the bounded-pose class, but this lower bound alone cannot establish sharpness for the larger class.

The same argument gives a generic efficiency certificate, following the optimal-recovery connection \citep{donoho1994,armstrong2018}. Let $U=\mathcal F_H(w)$ and $L>0$ be a feasible fixed-pose dual value, with $(U-L)/U\le\varepsilon$. Scaling its dual vector by $\kappa=z_{1-\alpha}/z_{1-\alpha/2}$ makes it feasible for the testing bound. Thus the optimum deterministic half-length $q^*$ obeys $q^*\ge\kappa L$, whereas the reported half-width satisfies $q\le U$. Consequently
\begin{equation}
\frac{q}{q^*}\le\frac{z_{1-\alpha/2}}{z_{1-\alpha}(1-\varepsilon)}.
\end{equation}
For $\alpha=0.05$ and $\varepsilon=0.005$, this is 1.198. The comparison applies to the continuous fixed-pose class; a post-hoc pose enlargement has no such gap certificate.


\subsection{Gaussian density-energy Gram matrix}
For isotropic paired features, let
\begin{align}
P_{jk}&=\left(\frac{2\pi\sigma_j^2\sigma_k^2}{\sigma_j^2+\sigma_k^2}\right)^{3/2}
\exp\left[-\frac{\norm{\mu_j-\mu_k}^2}{2(\sigma_j^2+\sigma_k^2)}\right],\\
Q_{jk}&=P_{jk}\text{ with }\mu_j-\mu_k\text{ replaced by }\mu_j+\mu_k.\nonumber
\end{align}
Integrating products of Gaussians and applying Parseval gives real and imaginary coefficient blocks $2(P+Q)$ and $2(P-Q)$, with zero cross block. A zero-centered imaginary coefficient is exactly null. Numerically excluded eigenmodes are reported; exclusion changes the stated Gaussian subspace. In an ambient audit they remain available to the larger space rather than being silently assigned zero uncertainty.

\subsection{Uniform nonlinear remainder}
For a Gaussian of width $\sigma$ at radial distance $r$, bounds on value, gradient and Hessian are
\begin{align}
g_0(r)&=e^{-r^2/(2\sigma^2)},\\
g_1(r)&=r g_0(r)/\sigma^2,\\
g_2(r)&=(1+r^2/\sigma^2)g_0(r)/\sigma^2.
\end{align}
On an interval of possible distances, $g_0$ is largest at the lower endpoint; $g_1$ and $g_2$ attain their largest value at the point nearest $\sigma$. A rotation radius $a\le\pi$ gives displacement at most $2\norm{k}\sin(a/2)$, speed at most $a\norm{k}$ and acceleration at most $a^2\norm{k}$. A shift radius $s$ gives phase speed at most $2\pi\norm{q}s/\text{box}$.

The product rule bounds the second derivative by the sum of squared-speed times $g_2$, acceleration times $g_1$, twice speed times phase-speed times $g_1$, and squared phase-speed times $g_0$. Sum conjugate-pair bounds and apply the fixed CTF/noise scale. A coefficient-column Frobenius bound controls the operator term; the pilot-weighted bound controls its pilot term. Taylor's integral formula gives Equation~\eqref{eq:pose}. Evaluating a Hessian only at the nominal pose would not establish this uniform result.

\subsection{Retaining quadratic pose terms}\label{sec:curvature}
The isotropic quadratic remainder can dominate an interval. A development extension retains the curvature and uses a cubic remainder instead. Put $E_{iab}=\partial^2 A_i/\partial u_{ia}\partial u_{ib}$ at zero, $Q_i(w)_{ab}=w_i^\top E_{iab}\rho_0$, and let $U_i(w)$ collect columns $E_{iab}^\top w_i$ over all ordered pairs. Since $\norm{u_i\otimes u_i}_F\le1$, a valid bias bound is
\begin{align}
b_2(w)&=B\norm{\ell-\A^*w}+\sum_i\norm{J_i^\top w_i}\nonumber\\
&\quad+B\sum_i\norm{T_i(w)}_F+\tfrac12\sum_i\norm{Q_i(w)}_F\nonumber\\
&\quad+\tfrac B2\sum_i\norm{U_i(w)}_F+\sum_i\gamma_{3i}\norm{w_i}.
\end{align}
For a Fourier voxel column, define $T(q,x)=2\pi a^3\norm{k}\norm{x}/d$. Its third derivative along any allowed pose segment is bounded by $|C|[L^3+3LH+T]$ before whitening. The three summands follow by differentiating the phase exponential three times. Pilot contraction and a column Frobenius bound give $H_{3i}$ and $K_{3i}$, hence $\gamma_{3i}=(H_{3i}+BK_{3i})/6$. Taylor's integral remainder and Cauchy--Schwarz then give Theorem~\ref{thm:coverage} with $b_2$. Sharing of density errors and rank-one quadratic pose constraints are still relaxed.

Analytic column Hessians and pixel-space Gram factors implement these Euclidean groups. Mixed finite differences and independent nonlinear projections test the implementation. The full three-geometry, two-target comparison is shown in Figure~\ref{fig:curvature}. The spectral post-audit follows Equation~\eqref{eq:shared}; its proof is the operator-norm bound applied to the concatenated vectors $[\sqrt{d_j}v_j]_j$, whose squared norm is at most $\sum_jd_j$.

\subsection{An independent noise-scale upper bound}
Known noise is not necessary if an independently calibrated upper bound is available. A standard construction assumes independent calibration coordinates $z_j=\mu_j+\sigma e_j$, $e_j\sim N(0,1)$, with arbitrary fixed means and independent inference noise. For $\nu$ coordinates, set
\begin{equation}
\overline\sigma=\norm{z}/\sqrt{\chi^2_{\nu,\beta}}.
\end{equation}
The probability that $\overline\sigma\ge\sigma$ is at least $1-\beta$: the central chi-square pivot is exact at zero mean, and a noncentral chi-square has a smaller lower tail. Conditional on calibration, weights may use $\overline\sigma$. On this event their inference standard deviation is at most $s_+=\overline\sigma\norm{w}$. For $\alpha<1/2$, $q(s_+,b,\alpha)\ge b$, and Gaussian coverage decreases with the actual noise standard deviation up to $s_+$. Integrating the conditional bound yields coverage at least $(1-\alpha)(1-\beta)\ge1-\alpha-\beta$.

This extension does not justify independence of windowed Fourier pixels or estimate arbitrary covariance. With 128 calibration coordinates and $\beta=0.01$, a 20,000-draw development control gives scale-bound failure 0.0103 under independence. Equicorrelation 0.5 raises that failure to 0.503. Such a calibration set violates the premise even if a particular resulting interval remains conservative. Experimental whitening remains unresolved.

\subsection{Simultaneous targets and sensitivity budgets}
For $K$ targets and parameter-budget choices fixed independently of inference noise, applying Theorem~\ref{thm:coverage} with $\alpha/K$ gives simultaneous coverage by the union bound. Correlated Gaussian maxima can reduce the noise component if the joint weight covariance is accounted for. Selecting the most favorable point on a sensitivity surface after inspecting inference data requires such simultaneous control. A density radius supplied by simulation truth is an oracle control and cannot be reported as a practical calibration method.

\section{Survey and Experimental Audit Trail}
The repository's living survey distinguishes density uncertainty, pose uncertainty, conformational variability/populations, map-processing confidence and atomic-model uncertainty. Its broad Europe PMC retrieval ledger contains 16,969 deduplicated records, mostly application papers; that count is not a count of reviewed methods. The initial survey checkpoint contained 89 candidate works; the current ledger contains 119, with local full-text availability recorded separately. Recent items include LocScale-2.0, CryoDiff, CryoSplat, GEM, GaussianEM, measurement-limited heterogeneity, population-counting work and the CAHRA dataset-construction preprint. Preprints and final publications are distinguished, and titles/versions are checked against primary sources.

The full experimental protocol draft records unimplemented comparisons and required failure controls. Fixed-pose resampling must be distinguished from rerunning alignment; a mathematical diagonal-Gaussian control must be distinguished from reproducing Ullrich et al.'s full software; cryoDRGN backprojection must be distinguished from training a neural model. Exposure-group splits do not undo the dependence introduced by full-data consensus poses. All development results, including the initially incomplete covariance-factor benchmark, remain identifiable by filename and status.
\section{Continuous Fourier Audit}\label{sec:continuous}
The sinc identity follows by integrating the two conjugate expansions of a real Fourier field. For a target component with center $\mu$ and width $\sigma_\ell$, the one-dimensional truncated transform is
\begin{align}
&e^{-2\pi i k\mu-2\pi^2\sigma_\ell^2 k^2}\nonumber\\
&\quad\times\big[\Phi((1/2-\mu)/\sigma_\ell+2\pi i\sigma_\ell k)\nonumber\\
&\hspace{48pt}-\Phi((-1/2-\mu)/\sigma_\ell+2\pi i\sigma_\ell k)\big].
\end{align}
Products over coordinates and sums over target components give $\A\ell$. Gaussian-product identities give $\norm{\ell}^2$. Independent tensor quadrature verifies these analytic expressions, while an independent conic problem formed from the joint Hilbert Gram of $[\ell,\A_1^*,\ldots,\A_m^*]$ checks optimization and dual feasibility.

For $r$ Gauss--Legendre nodes on a unit interval, the classical error constant is $C_r=(r!)^4/[(2r+1)((2r)!)^3]$ \citep{dlmfquadrature}. Put $M_a=\max_j|k_{ja}|$ and $E_r=\sum_a C_r(4\pi M_a)^{2r}$. Positive tensor weights $\omega_t$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
\left|\norm{f_w}^2-\sum_t\omega_t f_w(x_t)^2\right|
\le E_r\left(\sum_j|c_j|\right)^2.
\end{equation}
To verify the bound, expand $f_w^2$ into exponentials with coordinate frequencies at most $2M_a$. Apply the one-dimensional derivative remainder and telescope the three integration/quadrature operators. Their positive weights have total mass one, and the expansion's coefficient absolute sum is bounded by $(\sum_j|c_j|)^2$.

Similarly, the error in the continuous Gram action obeys
\begin{equation}
\norm{(G-G_Q)w}\le\sqrt2 E_r\norm{C/\sigma}_2\sum_j|c_j|.
\end{equation}
Adding the scalar remainder to the squared primal residual and the vector remainder to the dual feasibility denominator preserves the continuous objective bracket in real arithmetic. Two type-3 NUFFTs apply $G_Q$. At frequency radius five, quadrature order 40 makes the analytic kernel remainder at most $3.21\times10^{-23}$ before coefficient scaling. This quadrature theorem does not certify FINUFFT's floating-point error; requested tolerance $10^{-12}$ is checked numerically against independent sinc Grams.

An orthonormal constant-cell coefficient is scaled by the square root of inverse cell volume. Its exact transform includes $\prod_a\operatorname{sinc}(k_a/d)$ and a half-cell phase relative to the centered FFT grid. This prevents a point-sample approximation from being mislabeled exact continuous physics. For an orthogonal cell projection $P$, $\norm{h}^2=\norm{Ph}^2+\norm{(I-P)h}^2$. The omitted component is the continuous analogue of the omitted-dictionary term, and can affect coverage even when successive grid widths look similar.

\subsection{Continuous nonlinear-pose bound}\label{sec:continuouspose}
\begin{proposition}
Let $H=L^2([-1/2,1/2]^3)$, $\norm{\rho-\rho_0}\le B$, $P=\norm{\rho_0}$ and $\norm{u_i}\le1$. Put $h=A_0^*w-\ell$. Suppose $L_{12}$ bounds the continuous norm of the sum of first and half-scaled second pose-adjoint fields. For rotation radius $a$ and shift radius $s_t$ in field units, define $R=\sqrt3/2$ and
\begin{align}
L_{iq}&=2\pi(a\norm{k_{iq}}R+s_t\norm{q}),\\
H_{iq}&=2\pi a^2\norm{k_{iq}}R,\quad
T_{iq}=2\pi a^3\norm{k_{iq}}R,\\
K_{3i}^2&=\sum_q|C_{iq}/\sigma|^2(L_{iq}^3+3L_{iq}H_{iq}+T_{iq})^2.
\end{align}
Then Equation~\eqref{eq:continuouspose} bounds the scalar bias for every density and pose in the class. Combined with Theorem~\ref{thm:coverage}, it yields a continuous-density interval under the same independent Gaussian-noise assumptions.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The exact bias splits as $\ip{h}{\delta}+\ip{(A(u)^*-A_0^*)w}{\rho_0+\delta}$. The first term is at most $B\norm h$. The second pairs a Taylor polynomial plus remainder with a density of norm at most $P+B$. Along a rotation segment, the first three frequency-derivative norms are bounded by $a\norm k$, $a^2\norm k$ and $a^3\norm k$; the shift phase is linear. The third exponential derivative is bounded by $L^3+3LH+T$. The cube has unit volume, so summing squared column bounds gives the realified Hilbert--Schmidt bound $K_{3i}$. Taylor's integral remainder contributes $1/6$; Cauchy--Schwarz with $w_i$ completes the bound.
\end{proof}
To compute $L_{12}$, use orthonormal symmetric vectorization of the Hessian: diagonal entries are unchanged and off-diagonals are multiplied by $\sqrt2$. Its coefficient vector has norm $\norm{u_i}^2\le1$, so fifteen columns replace twenty-five ordered columns exactly. The derivative fields are polynomials of degree at most two times Fourier exponentials. Their Gram products have coordinate degrees at most four.

For quadrature order $r$, $n=2r$, and $\omega_a=4\pi\max_j|k_{ja}|$, define
\begin{align}
D_\beta(\omega)&=\sum_{l=0}^{\min(\beta,n)}\binom nl\frac{\beta!}{(\beta-l)!}
2^{l-\beta}\omega^{n-l},\\
E_{\rm poly}&=C_r\sum_a\max_{0\le\beta\le4}D_\beta(\omega_a).
\end{align}
Leibniz differentiation, the positive Gauss remainder kernel and tensor telescoping bound each monomial-exponential integration error by $E_{\rm poly}$. If column $j$ has absolute polynomial-Fourier coefficient sum at most $M_j$, the Gram-entry error is at most $E_{\rm poly}M_jM_l$ and its operator norm at most $E_{\rm poly}\sum_jM_j^2$. After dividing each column and its $M_j$ by $\sqrt{d_{\mathrm{block}(j)}}$, add that error to the largest quadrature-Gram eigenvalue in Equation~\eqref{eq:shared}. Separate and joint spectral bounds, and a padded Frobenius triangle bound, each remain valid; their minimum supplies $L_{12}$.

This integrates known adjoint fields, not the unknown density. Direct nonlinear Fourier fields check derivatives and cubic remainders independently. Exact constant-cell projections test reference generators. At a fixed nonzero pose, sinc integrals give the feasible worst bias over the entire continuous density ball, $|\ip{\rho_0}{(A(u)^*-A_0^*)w}|+B\norm{A(u)^*w-\ell}$; it is a lower bound on the global pose supremum. Floating-point and NUFFT tolerances remain numerical checks rather than validated arithmetic.

\subsection{Integrated-moment cubic remainder}\label{sec:momentremainder}
The earlier $K_{3i}$ uses the maximum spatial radius everywhere. A sharper scalar-estimator bound integrates known Fourier columns over the cube. Put
\begin{equation}
c_2=12^{-1/2},\quad c_4=(3/12^2)^{1/4},\quad c_6=(15/12^3)^{1/6}.
\end{equation}
For any $v\in\R^3$, $\norm{v^\top x}_{L^p(D)}\le c_p\norm{v}$ for $p=2,4,6$. To see this, expand the even moment under unit-volume cube integration. Odd-coordinate terms vanish, and each remaining term is nonnegative. The coordinate moments $1/12$, $1/80$ and $1/448$ are bounded by those of a centered Gaussian with variance $1/12$. Termwise comparison gives the stated constants. This is an integration identity for the operator, not a Gaussian model of the density.

\begin{proposition}[Integrated scalar remainder]
Under Proposition~2's density and pose classes, define
\begin{align}
L_p(k,q)&=2\pi(a\norm{k}c_p+s_t\norm{q}),\\
H_p(k)&=2\pi a^2\norm{k}c_p,\qquad T_2(k)=2\pi a^3\norm{k}c_2,\\
D(k,q)&=L_6(k,q)^3+3L_4(k,q)H_4(k)+T_2(k).
\end{align}
For $c_{iq}=(w_{R,iq}+iw_{I,iq})C_{iq}/\sigma$, the uniform scalar cubic remainder is bounded by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:momentremainder}
r_{\rm mom}(w)=\frac{B+P}{6}\sum_{i,q}|c_{iq}|D(k_{iq},q).
\end{equation}
It can replace the cubic term in Equation~\eqref{eq:continuouspose}, preserving the same conditional coverage guarantee.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Along a normalized pose segment, the phase $\phi(t,x)$ has derivative norms $\norm{\phi'}_p\le L_p$, $\norm{\phi''}_p\le H_p$ and $\norm{\phi'''}_2\le T_2$. This follows from $\norm{k^{(j)}(t)}\le a^j\norm{k}$ and linear detector translation. The exact third derivative of $e^{i\phi}$ is $e^{i\phi}(i\phi'''-3\phi'\phi''-i(\phi')^3)$. Minkowski and H\"older give its $L^2$ norm at most $D$. Triangle inequality with the fixed coefficients $c_{iq}$, Taylor's integral weight $1/6$, and Cauchy--Schwarz against a density of norm at most $B+P$ prove Equation~\eqref{eq:momentremainder}. Moreover, $\sum_q|c_{iq}|D_{iq}\le\norm{w_i}(\sum_q|C_{iq}/\sigma|^2D_{iq}^2)^{1/2}$, so retaining actual weights is no worse than this integrated Hilbert--Schmidt bound.
\end{proof}
The refinement applies classical inequalities to the Fourier-slice operator. Independent numerical integration checks the moment envelope, matrix-exponential derivatives, and exact nonlinear remainder. Those tests complement the proof; they are not exhaustive verification or validated arithmetic. The first development evaluation uses all original weights without refitting. A subsequent frozen protocol reserves different acquisition subsets and new signal/noise seeds for the one- and two-degree settings.

