\subsection{Experimental reconstruction and prediction context}
We execute stock cryoDRGN 4.3.1 homogeneous neural training on both exposure-group halves of all three stacks: three hidden layers, width 256, batch size eight, fixed consensus poses and CTFs. These are actual neural fits; they are not ab initio inference or uncertainty estimates. Gaussian and voxel ridge fits use the same halves. All methods are evaluated on identical held-out particles and 1,410 Fourier pairs per image inside radius 30 of the 64-pixel grid, with the same window and image-background normalization. Neural training itself uses the stock radius-32 loss and per-half global scaling, so training objectives are not identical.

Table~\ref{tab:reconstruction} selects checkpoints using tuning-group error only, from runs through 60 epochs for EMPIAR-10028/10049 and 100 epochs for EMPIAR-10076. The selected epochs are 60, 50 and 100. Two are the last available checkpoints, so undertraining remains possible. The development plateau check motivated these budgets; it is not a preregistered stopping rule or a proof of global convergence. Exposure-group bootstrap comparisons, checkpoint hashes and direct-network versus map-Fourier checks are recorded with the results. FSC assesses conditional map agreement; neither it nor image prediction labels true experimental density coverage.
\input{uncertainty-reconstruction-results.tex}

A frozen follow-up evaluates these unchanged models on 4,096 additional particles per dataset from exposure groups absent from development (Table~\ref{tab:freshprediction}). Neural prediction has lower NMSE than both controls in all three datasets; all six prespecified paired group-bootstrap differences remain positive under the stated multiplicity adjustment. This confirms conditional prediction ranking on new exposures, not experimental density coverage or independent-pose FSC.

Post-hoc background diagnostics on all 12,288 new images show mean nearest-neighbor normalized pair products of 0.197--0.292 across the three stacks and two image axes (Figure~\ref{fig:background}). White-Gaussian controls with the same background centering and normalization are near zero. The annular background can contain molecular signal, ice and preprocessing artifacts, so this is not identification of the measurement-noise law. It does show why simple spatial scaling cannot be assumed to establish whitening; our supplied-noise simulations do not resolve that experimental issue.


